  "semantic_claims": [
    {
      "claim_id": "majority-ground",
      "proposition": "The Court holds, under ordinary Twombly/Iqbal plausibility principles rather than any ERISA-specific heightened rule, that where an imprudence claim relies on a fund's relative underperformance, the plaintiff must compare it to a meaningful benchmark: an alternative sharing similar investment objectives and risk/reward aims (not identical holdings or asset allocation), since otherwise a difference in strategy is the obvious lawful explanation for the performance gap. It finds the Ninth Circuit applied that standard correctly to comparators (equity-heavy funds, S&P 500, 60/40 funds) with different aims."
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "ground-breadth",
      "proposition": "The holding is confined to claims premised on underperformance: the Court expressly says a meaningful benchmark is not required for imprudence theories that do not rely on comparative performance (e.g. attacks on the objective itself or an allocation facially inconsistent with the stated objective), says the comparator need not match asset allocation, and declines to decide whether underperformance can ever suffice on its own to show imprudence."
    }
  ],
